-The eviction policy is still open. Candidates are LRU, frequency-based eviction with aging, and 2Q. LRU has little bookkeeping and follows recent work quickly but is vulnerable to sequential scans. Aged frequency counts protect repeatedly popular clips but add counter maintenance and can lag a project switch. 2Q separates first-use entries from reused entries, resisting scans at the cost of extra queues and a queue-size parameter. None changes the external interface, queue concurrency, or disk budget.
+## Replacement Policy: 2Q
+
+Replacement uses 2Q, so a long sequential screening of one-use sheets cannot push out the sheets editors return to for comparison. The worker keeps three structures, all private to it:
+
+- **Probation queue**: a FIFO of resident sheets that have been admitted but not requested again. Its byte budget is a fixed fraction of the 12 GiB bound.
+- **Protected queue**: an LRU of resident sheets that were requested again after admission. It holds the remainder of the bound.
+- **Ghost list**: a bounded FIFO of cache keys (no JPEG data) recently evicted from probation.
+
+A completed sheet enters probation, or enters protected directly when its key is on the ghost list. A cache hit on a probation sheet moves it to protected; a hit on a protected sheet refreshes its recency. Eviction takes the oldest probation sheet while probation exceeds its budget, and otherwise the least recently used protected sheet. Ghost entries are keys only and do not count against the 12 GiB bound.
+
+The policy changes neither the external interface, the render queue's concurrency, nor the disk budget.
+
+## Decisions & Alternatives
+
+| Decision | Chosen | Alternatives Considered | Rationale |
+|----------|--------|------------------------|-----------|
+| Replacement policy | 2Q | LRU; frequency-based eviction with aging | Editors alternate long sequential screenings with repeated comparison of a small working set. 2Q keeps reused sheets from being evicted by one-use scans, which LRU does not; aged frequency counts keep stale favorites too long after a project switch. The cost is two queues and a tunable size split, all inside the worker. |
